@ajs, 2026-10-17: first "Let's keep (e), (f), and (g)". Then, after asking whether agents will be able to do these in five years, and hearing that (e) is the weakest against better agents (judging whether a requirement is well posed is judgement an agent will do better than a field-presence check): "let's keep g and f, but remove e".

KEPT AS INTENT (not yet requirements; none is written until he says so):
- (f) sampled evidence records k, how the sample was drawn and δ; reflow2 computes the bound and always labels it evidence, never proof. This is jobs 3 and 4 of dec:idea-reflow2-does-fewer-things-and-does-them-really-well: checks that don't reason and that anyone can re-run.
- (g) a failure budget only the owner sets, with what has been spent checked against it. This is job 5: decisions stay the owner's. It is about authority, not about what an agent can do.

DROPPED: (e) a requirement posed as a well-posed task. Why, in short: its lasting value was only a shallow presence check, and agents will judge well-posedness better.

CONSIDERED, NOT CHOSEN:
- (a) the designer never grades its own work;
- (b) distinct approaches first;
- (c) a trade-off frontier;
- (d) an archive of attempts.

